  "_comment": [
    "RECORDED, NOT CONSTRUCTED. These 52 rows are the licensed Datafeedr response to",
    "a brand-column query for James Perse, fetched into production on 2026-08-13 at",
    "15:20 UTC and read back out of `catalog_lookups` rather than re-requested — so",
    "this fixture cost ZERO metered requests against the 10,000/month plan.",
    "",
    "They answer the question tests/fixtures/catalogue-families.json could not: the",
    "licensed feed DOES carry James Perse, 52 rows across three merchants, and the",
    "launch family (Combed Cotton-Jersey T-Shirt) really does arrive as seven",
    "separate merchant rows that have to be folded into one card.",
    "",
    "WHAT IS VERBATIM: externalId, title, colour, sourceHost, and whether the row",
    "had an image, a thumbnail and an identifier. Those are every field the grouping",
    "and ranking rules read.",
    "",
    "WHAT IS NOT: imageUrl, linkUrl and directUrl are synthesised from sourceHost",
    "and externalId. The real ones are merchant affiliate tracking links, they are",
    "long, they rot, and no rule under test looks at anything but whether they are",
    "present. priceMinor is zeroed because no rule may read it and the handoff bans",
    "price from every surface.",
    "",
    "`category` and `gender` are null on every row. That is not a simplification —",
    "it is what the cache actually holds, because these rows were written by the",
    "deployed pre-Packet-1 code, which had no such fields. It makes this fixture the",
    "realistic worst case for grouping: a title, a colour, and nothing else.",
    "",
    "THREE RULES WERE WRONG UNTIL THIS FIXTURE EXISTED, and each was invisible in",
    "the constructed one:",
    "  1. `distressed`/`vintage`/`washed`/`faded` were dropped from the family key,",
    "     so Mr Porter's Distressed Cotton-Jersey T-Shirt merged into the plain",
    "     Cotton-Jersey T-Shirt and its Brown appeared as a colourway of a garment",
    "     nobody had looked at.",
    "  2. Mr Porter formats every title \"Brand - Product - Men - Colour - Size\", so",
    "     stripping colour and size left every card reading \"... T-Shirt - Men\".",
    "  3. Grailed writes \"(Size L)\"; stripping \"L)\" left names ending \"... (Size\"."
